# PRD PREFIX

## 0. The Discovery

The web already contains the beginnings of a visitor preference system.

Browsers and operating systems can currently communicate preferences including:

- reduced motion
- light/dark color scheme
- contrast preferences
- forced colors
- reduced transparency
- input capabilities such as touch, mouse and hover
- certain device/environment characteristics

Website developers can listen for these signals and alter the experience accordingly.

Yet there is a fundamental disconnect:

### Websites have been taught to listen.

### Users were never taught how to speak.

Millions of websites contain code capable of reacting to preferences that many ordinary people do not know they have, do not know where to configure, and may not even realize websites can detect.

The controls are fragmented among:

- browser settings
- operating-system settings
- accessibility settings
- device settings
- individual website controls

SeeItMyWay starts by making this invisible layer visible.

Before asking users to install anything, create an account, or adopt a new preference standard, SeeItMyWay should answer one extremely simple question:

> **What is my browser already telling websites about how I prefer the web?**

That becomes the front door to the product.

---

# 0.1 The SeeItMyWay Flywheel

SeeItMyWay should not begin by asking people to understand a new protocol.

It should progressively reveal that a partial protocol already exists.

```text
DISCOVER
↓
See what your browser already tells websites.

UNDERSTAND
↓
See what each preference actually changes.

CONFIGURE
↓
Learn where to change existing preferences.

EXPERIENCE
↓
Compare different versions of the same website.

EXPAND
↓
Add preferences browsers don't currently support.

CARRY
↓
Use the optional Preference Passport across sites.

ADAPT
↓
Participating websites respond to those preferences.
```

The consumer journey teaches the product while simultaneously demonstrating its value.

---

# 0.2 Revised Product Thesis

Traditional web personalization attempts to understand the visitor.

SeeItMyWay attempts to understand the visitor's **instructions**.

Traditional personalization:

```text
Observe me
↓
Track me
↓
Profile me
↓
Guess what I want
```

SeeItMyWay:

```text
Ask me
↓
Listen
↓
Respond
```

The system should use explicit preferences whenever possible instead of behavioral inference.

The visitor does not need to explain *why* they want an experience a certain way.

They simply express:

> Less motion.

> Larger controls.

> No autoplay.

> More room.

> More detail.

> Dark interface.

> Simpler navigation.

The website responds.

---

# 0.3 The Missing Consumer Layer

Existing preference standards are primarily documented for developers.

SeeItMyWay should introduce them to ordinary internet users.

The company therefore operates across three layers:

## Layer A — Existing Web Preferences

Preferences browsers and operating systems already communicate.

SeeItMyWay discovers, explains and demonstrates them.

---

## Layer B — SeeItMyWay Preferences

Additional preferences not currently standardized by browsers.

Examples:

- interface density
- content detail
- instruction style
- visual richness
- autoplay behavior
- navigation complexity

These can initially be stored locally and eventually carried through the optional Preference Passport.

---

## Layer C — Adaptive Websites

Participating sites interpret the available preference layer and provide appropriate experience variants.

SeeItMyWay therefore connects:

> **Human preference → machine-readable signal → website behavior**

